WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.800
Laughter is a social emotion,

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.680
so we're 30 times
more likely to laugh

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.440
if there is somebody else with
us than if we're on our own.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.400
And we'll laugh more
if we know those people,

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.440
and we'll laugh more
if we like those people.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.760
Why do humans laugh?

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.480
We laugh to show that we agree
with what someone said.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.360
That we remember the same
thing that they're alluding to.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.920
People will also use laughter
to try and mask other emotions.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.600
People will laugh to try
and pretend they're not upset

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.240
or to cover up being angry
or embarrassed or in pain.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.920
People will use laughter
to get other people to do things.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.440
If you get someone laughing,

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.280
they will tell you more intimate
details about themselves.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.520
There are two main kinds
of laughter, according to Sophie.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.720
The first is sort of
spontaneous laughter,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.960
if you think about laughter
that happens

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.680
and you cannot stop laughing,
it can sound like this:

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.480
The laughter just overwhelms you

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.000
and then you're just going
to have to ride it out.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.840
That's quite a different kind
of laughter

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.440
from the sort of laughter
you find in conversations,

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.920
which is of course most
of the laughter you encounter,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.440
and that sounds more like this.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.400
There laughter is really brief
and it's shared,

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.760
people tend to laugh together,

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.400
and they laugh together
in a very coordinated way.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.240
People laugh at the ends
of sentences.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:24.920
And that's actually
quite interesting,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.040
because it's even the case

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.160
if somebody's having a sign
language conversation

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.600
where they could laugh
all the way through,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.480
they don’t, they still laugh together
at the ends of sentences.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.840
So why can laughter
sometimes feel contagious?

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.440
You can probably think of occasions

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.080
when you've been watching television
or listening to the radio,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.080
maybe it's been broadcast live,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.640
and the presenter or presenters
start to get the giggles.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.720
The award-winning screenwriter, Abby
Mann, has died at the age of 80.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:56.200
He won an Academy Award in 1961
for Judgement at Nuremberg.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.680
Ab-- Excuse me, sorry.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.240
Abby Mann also won
several Emmys, including--

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.240
Including one in 1973 for--

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.520
For a film which featured a--
(giggling)

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.360
A police detective called--
(laughter)

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.200
If you chuckled while listening
to that clip, here's why.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.760
It's called behaviourally
contagious phenomena,

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.760
which are things you can catch
from somebody else

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.480
just because they're doing them.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.640
You might have noticed
this happens with yawning,

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.360
it happens a lot with laughter.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.440
Babies don't show any behaviourally
contagious phenomena,

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.040
and effectively we teach them to.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.240
So if you look at babies and parents,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.360
parents will try
and make babies laugh,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.360
and babies do laugh
and it's lovely.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.360
If the parent laughs,
the baby doesn't join in.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.320
But if the baby laughs,
the parent does join in,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.400
because the contagion is working.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.400
And effectively that seems
to be the root

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.160
whereby we start to teach babies

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.040
that this is something
that we do together.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.800
Laughter isn't exclusive to humans.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:03.120
We know that laughter is extremely
easily observed in other apes.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.960
So gorillas laugh, chimpanzees laugh,
orangutans laugh,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.640
and they laugh in a very, very
similar way to humans.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.880
The main difference is that
chimpanzees, for example,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.800
laugh on an exhalation
and an inhalation,

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.880
so we just laugh ha-ha-ha,
breathing out,

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.400
chimpanzees do more
like a ha-ah-ha-ah,

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.000
sounds a bit like he-eh-he-eh.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.920
So chimpanzees use laughter
in a pretty complex way

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.560
much as we do, and it happens
in the same situations.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.840
It's associated with
tickling, with play,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.720
with trying to make play last longer.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.920
And it's not just primates.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.400
Laughter-like behaviour has also been
described in rats and parrots.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.160
Unfortunately laughter
can also have a darker side.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.200
These big squeezes that you do with
your ribcage when you laugh,

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.400
particularly if you're laughing
really hard,

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.840
it's quite stressful for your heart
and your lungs

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.480
and that can mean if you have some
sort of problem

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.720
maybe with your heart or your lungs
or your blood vessels,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.720
you can put them under more strain.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.920
And throughout history
there are examples of people

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.880
who have died laughing.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.080
And I think, if you're going
to have to go somehow,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.240
probably going while you're laughing,
there could be worse ways.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.840
Luckily there are lots of health
benefits to laughing too.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.920
You are more relaxed when you laugh.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.560
So as soon as you start laughing,
you get a reduction in adrenaline.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.040
You also get an increased uptake

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.600
of the body's naturally
circulating endorphins.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.840
Now that is because you do quite a
lot of exercise at your ribcage

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.040
when you laugh.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.600
It's exactly the same
as a runner's high

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.680
or the good feeling
you get after exercising.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.200
And interestingly
that's true of laughter

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.040
even if you've
completely faked the laughter,

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.520
so it does suggest that
it's something to do

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.680
with the ha-ha-ha movements.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.320
So if you want to get
your endorphins going,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.120
you can start
by laughing out loud.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.200
And if you're by yourself
and have no one to laugh with,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.560
here's something that might help.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.440
Oh my god.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.320
Oh!

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.080
Where did you find that?

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.920
What is that?

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.240
(laughing)

